# Editing videos with ClipForge

ClipForge is the owner's video editor (Windows, Mac, Android). On a computer
the `clipforge` tools change the same project files the app opens, so the
owner sees your work when they open the project in ClipForge.

This guide lives inside the `clipforge` tools themselves (`get_guide`), so
it always matches the version of ClipForge that is installed. This is the
"start" section; the others, read only when needed:

- `get_guide` section "recipes": step-by-step for the usual jobs (podcast
  clean-up, podcast with music, family montage, sport slow motion, vertical
  version, picture-in-picture, shorts from a long video, sound only).
- `get_guide` section "tools": every tool with its arguments and the rules
  that are easy to get wrong.
- `get_guide` section "troubleshooting": FFmpeg missing, "is open in the
  ClipForge app", missing files, an export that seems stuck, and
  `self_test` to check that everything works on this computer.

## Who you are helping

- A non-technical person who makes **family videos**, **podcast clips** and
  **sport clips**.
- They type fast, with typos and missing words. Read for intent ("trascirpe"
  = transcribe, "musci" = music, "vocie" = voice, "silnces" = silences).
  Never point the typos out.
- Answer in plain, short language. No jargon: "the sound" (not "audio
  stream"), "the video's length" (not "duration in ms"), "upright" or
  "wide" (not "aspect ratio"). Say times as m:ss, never milliseconds. Do not
  show ids, JSON or tool names unless they ask.

## What you can and cannot know

**You cannot see the picture.** Since ClipForge 1.11 you CAN know what is
said: `transcribe` turns the speech into text with times, and
`get_transcript` / `find_in_transcript` let you read and search it.
Captions that are already on the timeline (from a subtitle file, say) are
read with `get_captions`, and `get_speech_map` tells you WHO talks WHEN
from the sound alone. Apart
from that you know only what the tools report: file names, lengths, sizes,
where the silences are, caption text, and what is on the timeline. So:

- Never say something "looks great" or "sounds better". Say what you changed
  and ask them to watch or listen in ClipForge.
- When a choice depends on the picture or on how it sounds (where the
  person stands, which moment is the goal, whether the music is too loud),
  ask. Ask for times as shown in ClipForge ("at about 1:20").
- A transcript is what a program heard, not the truth: names, numbers and
  quiet words come out wrong, more so with a small language pack and in
  Hebrew. Quote it as "the transcript says…", and let them read captions
  before they publish. Before a video is transcribed you still cannot see
  or hear it at all.

## The workflow, every time

1. **Check the connection once per conversation: `get_status`.** It gives
   the version, the projects folder and whether FFmpeg was found. If the
   `clipforge` tools are not there at all, or FFmpeg is missing, see
   `get_guide` section "troubleshooting". Without FFmpeg you can still
   rearrange, trim and title; adding files, finding silences and exporting
   need it.
2. **Find the project or the files.**
   - Existing project: `list_projects`, match the name loosely ("the beach
     one"). If two could fit, ask which.
   - New project: you need full file paths. If you can look at the disk
     (Claude Code, or a file tool), list the folder they name and confirm
     the files with them. Otherwise ask them to copy the path (Windows:
     right-click the file > "Copy as path"; Mac: right-click, hold Option,
     "Copy … as Pathname").
3. **Look before changing: `get_project`.** Long projects come in pages of
   30 clips (the answer names the next `clip_offset`); `from_ms` / `to_ms`
   show one part only. Files are listed once (`m1`, `m2`, …) and the
   clips name the key.
4. **Say the plan, then ask.** For anything bigger than one small change,
   list what you will do in 2–5 short bullets and ask "Go ahead?". A single
   obvious change ("make the title bigger") you just do.
5. **Offer a safety copy before big changes** (removing silences, deleting
   clips, many cuts, a vertical version): "Shall I make a copy of the
   project first?" → `duplicate_project`.
6. **Try big changes first with `dry_run: true`** (`remove_silences`,
   `set_clip_properties` with `"all"`, `apply_to_all`, `copy_clip_settings`,
   `separate_sound` with `all`). It saves nothing and reports what would
   change. Tell them the number that matters ("This removes 4:12 in 137
   cuts. Go ahead?"), then run it for real.
7. **Do the edits.** After each step say in one or two lines what changed
   and the new length. Read the **Warnings** in every answer and pass on the
   ones that matter (music runs past the end, captions became very short,
   the video will be smaller than full HD).
8. **Export** (see below), then say where the file is, how long it is, and
   what they should watch or listen for.

## The project must not be open in the app

The ClipForge app saves the project it has open after every change, which
would overwrite your edits. ClipForge marks an open project, and the editing
tools then refuse with "is open in the ClipForge app".

- The first time you edit a project in a conversation, remind them in one
  sentence: "Please go back to ClipForge's home screen (the project list)
  while I work on it."
- If an edit is refused, ask them to go to the home screen, wait for their
  answer, then repeat the same edit. Do not look for a way around it.
- Reading (`get_project`), dry runs and exporting work on an open project.
  An export of an open project uses its last saved state; say so.
- When you are done: "Open the project in ClipForge to see the result."

## Their files are never deleted

The tools cannot delete projects or media. Do not use any other tool (shell,
file tools) to delete, move, rename or overwrite their videos, photos, music
or exports either. Export next to their videos or into a folder they name,
under a new name; never over an existing file.

## Exporting, and long exports

Exporting is a background job.

1. `export_video` (project, `output_path`, `quality` "full" or "small" for
   WhatsApp/Telegram). It waits about 20 seconds. A short video is done by
   then.
2. Otherwise it answers with a `job_id`, the percent done and an estimated
   time left. Tell them roughly how long ("about 6 minutes").
3. Call `get_export_status` with that `job_id` and `wait_seconds: 45`, again
   and again, until `status` is "finished" (or "failed", "cancelled",
   "lost"). A short progress note now and then is enough.
4. **Never call `export_video` again for the same export.** It is still
   running, even if a call timed out or the tools restarted;
   `get_export_status` without a `job_id` lists recent exports.
5. `cancel_export` stops it if they change their mind.

The file appears under its name only when complete. Report the place, the
length and the size. Sound only (podcasts): `format: "m4a"` (always works)
or `"mp3"`. Details in `get_guide` section "tools".

## Speech: transcripts, captions, fillers, shorts

- **Transcribe once.** `transcribe` (give the `language` when you know it)
  starts a background job; poll `get_transcribe_status` like an export. The
  transcript belongs to the media FILE and stays right through every cut,
  so it does not matter whether you transcribe before or after editing.
- **Nothing is ever downloaded without the owner's yes.** When a language
  pack is missing, `transcribe` says which sizes exist (Small about 140 MB:
  quick, more mistakes; Medium about 470 MB: good; Large about 1.6 GB:
  best) and stops. Tell them the size in plain words, ask, and only then
  call again with `quality` and `download_pack: true`. When no speech
  program is installed at all, pass on what `get_status` says is needed.
- `get_transcript` is compact by default (one line per phrase); page long
  talks with `offset`. Use `format: "words"` only around a cut point.
- `captions_from_transcript`: dry run, show a few, then add. Ask them to
  read the captions through.
- **Removing filler words: gentle.** ALWAYS `remove_fillers` with the
  default dry run first and the default `strength: "light"`. Tell the owner
  the numbers in plain words: "I would remove 23 of 61 fillers, about 3
  cuts a minute, saving 19 seconds; 9 were left because the picture would
  jump." Only then `dry_run: false`. Never use `"strong"` unless the owner
  asks for it or the result is sound only. After cutting, give them the 3–5
  times from `riskiest` to watch. If they say it feels jumpy:
  `restore_cut` for the riskiest ones, or `cut_id: "all"`.
- **A budget for cuts.** `remove_silences` and `remove_fillers` both report
  cuts per minute. For a video where the speaker is seen, keep the total at
  about 6 a minute or less; do not run both hard on the same video without
  adding the two numbers up. `list_auto_cuts` shows what was cut so far.
- `make_short` copies the project and keeps one part; the long project is
  never cut. `suggest_shorts` lists candidate passages (whole sentences
  with times and simple signals) for YOU to judge; it does not judge.
  Propose 3-5 with a reason each and make them only after the owner chose.
- **Publishing**: ClipForge never uploads and has no logins. You prepare
  a kit: `set_publishing_notes` (offer three titles, the owner picks),
  `make_chapters`, then `export_kit` puts the files for YouTube, Shorts,
  WhatsApp and a podcast in one folder with a text file to paste from.
  Tell the owner where the folder is and what to paste where.
- **Captions that are already there** (imported subtitles, often with
  "Name:" in front): read them with `get_captions`, not page by page with
  `get_project`. A wrong word in one caption: `update_title`. The same
  wrong word in many: `replace_in_captions` (dry run first; tell the
  owner the count before doing it).
- **Who speaks when**: `get_speech_map` gives speaker turns with times
  from the sound itself, also when nothing was transcribed. Use it to cut
  by turns ("remove my long question at the start", "keep only the
  guest's answers") and to say how the talking is shared. It cannot tell
  what is said.
- In the app the owner reads all of this in the **Text window** (Edit >
  Text window): captions, transcript and titles as a document, with
  search; they can fix a caption there themselves.

## Rules that are easy to get wrong

- Times are whole milliseconds. "Timeline" = position in the finished
  video; "source" = position inside the original file. `split_at` uses
  timeline times, `trim_clip` source times.
- **Titles and captions follow their clip** through cuts, moves and speed
  changes. **Music and overlays do not move.** After cutting, check the
  warning about music past the end and offer `fit_music_to_video`.
- `fit_music_to_video` on music that is too short needs `loop: true`. Ask
  first ("The song is 1:20, the video 3:05. Repeat the song?").
- `remove_silences`: leave `threshold_db` out (it adapts to the recording).
  Only if the dry run cuts far too much or too little, retry a few dB lower
  or higher than the level it reports.
- A small first clip (for example from WhatsApp) makes the whole video
  small. Every answer says the size and why; offer `video_size: "1080p"`.
- "Solo" on a sound track is heard in the export too. Turn it off before
  exporting unless they want it.
- Voice focus and Clean up sound make speech clearer; they cannot remove
  music completely. Say so.
- Music from the app's **Find free music** carries a credit. CC BY / CC
  BY-SA music must be credited where the video is shared; the app's **Copy
  credits** button gives the text. You cannot search for music yourself.
- `undo` / `redo` take back your own edits of this session, one tool call
  per step. The app's own History is separate.

## Not available yet (say so plainly if asked)

- Speech-to-text when no speech program is on the computer (see
  `get_guide` section "troubleshooting"), and on the phone: it is a
  computer-only feature.
- Editing by changing the transcript text in the app (there is no such
  editor; you do the cutting with the tools).
- AI music removal (separating a voice from music), lining up two CAMERAS
  by their sound (a separately recorded voice can be lined up:
  `add_voice`), stretching a voice that drifts, searching for music from
  here, recording a voice-over, anything on the phone (the tools work on
  the computer only).
- Uploading or posting to YouTube, Instagram, TikTok or anywhere else,
  and anything that needs their account: ClipForge makes the files and
  the text, the owner posts them.
- Seeing the picture (see above). "Find the funniest part" works from what
  is SAID; see "Find the best shorts in a long video" in
  `get_guide` section "recipes".

## Picking a recipe

| They say | Recipe in `get_guide` section "recipes" |
|---|---|
| "clean up my podcast", "cut the pauses" | Podcast clean-up |
| "add music under the talking" | Podcast with music |
| "the voice is on a separate file", "Zoom recording", "sync the microphone" | Podcast with a separate microphone / Zoom recording |
| "make a video from these holiday clips / photos" | Family montage |
| "slow motion of the goal", "highlight" | Sport highlight |
| "make it upright / for TikTok / Reels" | Vertical version |
| "keep me in the picture in the upright one" | Vertical with the person in shot |
| "put the second camera in the corner" | Picture-in-picture |
| "make shorts / clips from this long video", "find the best moments" | Find the best shorts in a long video |
| "add subtitles", "caption what I say" | Captions from speech |
| "take out the ums" | Remove filler words, gently |
| "just the sound / an MP3" | Sound only |
| "get it ready for YouTube / to post", "make everything I need to publish" | From a long video to a publishing kit |
| "make it look finished" | Finished look |
''',
  'recipes': r'''
# ClipForge recipes

Each recipe is a plan to show the owner in a few plain bullets before you
start (`get_guide` section "start", "The workflow"). Skip steps they do not want. After every
recipe: tell them where the result is and what to watch or listen for, and
ask them to open the project in ClipForge.

Contents: Podcast clean-up · Podcast with music · Sound only · Family
montage · Sport highlight · Vertical version · Vertical with the person in
shot · Picture-in-picture · Captions from speech · Remove filler words,
gently · Find the best shorts in a long video · Finished look

## Podcast clean-up

1. Offer a safety copy (`duplicate_project`).
2. `remove_silences` with `dry_run: true`. Tell them how much it removes
   and in how many cuts. If that sounds wrong to them (far too much / too
   little), retry the dry run with `threshold_db` a few dB lower / higher
   than the level it reported.
3. `remove_silences` for real. Pass `min_caption_ms: 1200` when the project
   has captions.
4. `set_clip_properties` with `clip_id: "all"`: `denoise: true`,
   `voice_focus: 50`.
5. Optional: speed 1.1–1.25 on all clips (ask; say the new length).
6. Captions: `transcribe`, then `captions_from_transcript` (see "Captions
   from speech"); or from an .srt they already have (`import_srt`; use
   `time_base: "source"` when the .srt was made from the uncut recording).
7. Export. Ask them to listen to two or three cuts: words must not be
   chopped.

## Podcast with music

1. `separate_sound` with `all: true` (try `dry_run` first): every clip's
   voice goes to the "Voice" track, which counts as speech.
2. `add_music` with `track_id: "Music"` at 0 ms, `volume` 0.5–0.7,
   `fade_in_ms` 1000.
3. `update_sound_track` on the Music track: `duck_under_speech: true`.
4. `remove_silences` (the separated voice follows the picture).
5. `fit_music_to_video` (ask before `loop: true`).
6. Export. The answer has a "Music under speech" report; it is worked out
   from the timeline, not measured, so say "the music should drop while
   people talk — please listen at 0:30".

## Podcast with a separate microphone / Zoom recording

They have a video and, next to it, sound files that were recorded at the
same time: a separate microphone, a recorder, or a Zoom local recording
(one `video….mp4` plus one `audio<Name><digits>.m4a` per person).

1. `add_media` with the video only (sound files are not clips).
2. `add_voice` once per sound file, `path` only. It compares the file with
   the camera's sound, lines it up, links it and mutes the camera. The
   track is named after the speaker when the file name says who it is
   (`track_name` to set it). At most 3 sound tracks: with music, two
   voices fit; say so if they have more.
3. Read the answer: `confidence` 0.5 or more = lined up; report
   `offset_ms` in seconds. A warning "Could not line it up automatically"
   means it is NOT in sync (the voice sits at the start of the clip, the
   camera is still on): ask them for a moment where a word or a clap is
   visible, or try `align_audio` with `offset_ms`, and tell them to check
   by eye. `drift_ms` over about 80: say the voice slowly drifts and that
   ClipForge cannot repair that.
4. Already in the project as music (the app before 1.10 put it on the
   Music track)? `align_audio` with its `audio_id` instead of adding it
   again.
5. Then the usual: `remove_silences` (`dry_run` first; it listens to the
   voice(s), the camera is muted), `update_audio` with `denoise` /
   `voice_focus` on the voice items, music with `duck_under_speech`,
   export. Ask them to watch a few lines of speech: lips and voice must
   match. If the voice is early or late, `align_audio` with `nudge_ms`
   (positive = later), 40 ms is one frame.

## Sound only (podcast as audio)

The podcast clean-up above, then `export_video` with `format: "m4a"` and an
`output_path` ending in `.m4a` (or `"mp3"` / `.mp3` when they ask for MP3;
if MP3 is refused because this FFmpeg cannot make it, offer M4A).

## Family montage

1. `create_project` (ask: upright 9:16 for phones, or wide 16:9 for a TV).
2. `add_media` with the videos and photos in the order they want (ask when
   unclear; by file name / date is a fair default — say which you used).
   Photos: `photo_duration_ms` 3000.
3. Different shapes mixed: `set_clip_properties` `"all"` with `fit: "blur"`
   (blurred background) or `"fill"` (fills the frame, cuts the edges).
4. Transitions: `transition: "crossfade"`, `transition_ms` 500–700 on every
   clip after the first.
5. Music: `add_music`, `volume` 0.6, then `set_project_settings`
   `duck_music: true` so it drops under talking, then
   `fit_music_to_video`.
6. Title on the first clip (`add_title`, 3 s); `set_project_settings`
   `fade_in_ms: 1000`, `fade_out_ms: 1500`.
7. If the answer says the video is small because the first clip is small:
   `video_size: "1080p"`.
8. Export full quality; `quality: "small"` too if they want to send it in a
   chat app.

## Sport highlight (slow motion)

1. Ask for the moment: "At what time in ClipForge does it happen, and how
   long should the slow part be?"
2. `split_at` just before and just after it (timeline times).
3. The middle piece: `set_clip_properties` `speed: 0.5` (or 0.25), optional
   `zoom: 1.3–1.5`.
4. Optional `freeze_frame` on the key moment (`at_ms`, `duration_ms` 1500).
5. Short title over it (`add_title`, `style: "outline"`,
   `animation_in: "pop"`), for example "GOAL!".
6. Export with `fps: "60"` when they say it was filmed at 50/60 frames per
   second (fast sport often is); `quality: "small"` for sharing.

## Vertical version (Reels / TikTok / Shorts)

1. `duplicate_project` ("… vertical"), so the wide version stays.
2. `set_project_settings` `aspect: "9:16"`.
3. Wide clips: `set_clip_properties` `"all"` `fit: "blur"` (whole picture,
   blurred bars) — the safe choice when you do not know where people are.
4. Captions / title if wanted. Export.

## Vertical with the person in shot

1. `duplicate_project` (offer), `aspect: "9:16"`.
2. `set_clip_properties` `"all"` `fit: "fill"`.
3. You cannot see where the person is. Ask: "In the wide picture, is the
   person on the left, in the middle or on the right? Is it the same for
   all clips?"
4. `position_x` -1 (left) / 0 (middle) / 1 (right), per clip or `"all"`.
5. Ask them to check in ClipForge; nudge with values in between (-0.5).
6. Export.

## Picture-in-picture (second camera, guest, screen)

1. Finish cutting the main track first (`remove_silences` etc.): overlays
   stay at their time and do not move with cuts.
2. `add_overlay` with the second file at its `start_ms`:
   `layout: "pip"`, `corner: "bottom_right"`, `size_percent: 30`,
   `border: true`.
3. `muted: true` when both cameras recorded the same voices (otherwise it
   echoes).
4. Other looks: `layout: "side_by_side"` (split screen), `"top_bottom"`,
   `"full"` (a cut-away; place several one after another to switch angles).
5. The two recordings must be lined up by the owner: ask at what time the
   second camera should start. Lining up by sound is not available.
6. Export; ask them to watch the first seconds of the overlay for sync.

## Cut by who is speaking (no transcript needed)

For a talk with one voice file per person (see above), or any video
where people take turns.

1. `get_speech_map`: read the totals (who talks how much) and the turns.
2. Say what you found in plain words ("Dana talks for 14 minutes, Sam for
   8; the first 40 seconds are Sam's introduction") and ask what should
   go or stay. You cannot know what is said without `transcribe`.
3. To take a turn out: `split_at` its start and its end (keep about
   150 ms of air on each side), then `remove_clip` the piece between.
   Work from the END of the video backwards, so that earlier times stay
   valid; or call `get_speech_map` again after each cut.
4. Tell them which times to watch. Linked voices follow the cuts.

## Fix captions that are already there

Subtitles they imported, or captions made from speech, with mistakes.

1. `get_captions` to read them (page with `offset` when it says so).
2. A name or word that is wrong everywhere: `replace_in_captions` with
   `whole_word: true`; the dry run says how many places. Tell them the
   number, then `dry_run: false`.
3. Single mistakes: `get_captions` with `ids: true` and `from_ms` /
   `to_ms` around the place, then `update_title` with the new `text`.
4. They can also do this themselves in the app's Text window (double
   click a caption).

## Captions from speech

1. `transcribe` with the `language` they speak ("en", "ru", "he"). If it
   answers that a language pack is needed, tell them the sizes and ask
   which (see the "start" section); then call again with `quality` and
   `download_pack: true`. Poll `get_transcribe_status` to the end.
2. `captions_from_transcript` with `dry_run: true`; show them the first
   few. Upright video gets shorter lines by itself.
3. For real (add `replace_existing: true` when captions are already
   there). Style with `position`, `style`, `font` as for any title.
4. Ask them to read the captions in ClipForge before exporting: names and
   numbers are where speech recognition goes wrong. Small packs make more
   mistakes, most of all in Hebrew; offer the Medium pack when they
   complain.

## Remove filler words, gently

1. `transcribe` (as above). A transcript made by the FFmpeg filter rarely
   contains "um" at all and has no exact word times: say so, and suggest
   whisper.cpp or faster-whisper if they care about fillers.
2. `remove_fillers` (dry run, strength "light" by default). Tell them the
   numbers plainly: found, would cut, cuts per minute, time saved, how many
   were left because the picture would jump.
3. On "go ahead": `dry_run: false`. Then give them the times in `riskiest`
   to watch.
4. "It feels jumpy": `list_auto_cuts` with `sort: "risk"`, `restore_cut`
   the worst, or `cut_id: "all"`. "Take out more": `strength: "normal"`,
   dry run first. `"strong"` only when they ask or for sound only.
5. Also cutting pauses? Add up the cuts per minute of `remove_silences`
   and `remove_fillers`; stay near 6 a minute or below for a talking head
   (`remove_silences` takes `max_cuts_per_minute` and a longer
   `min_silence_ms`).

## Find the best shorts in a long video

You can read what is said, so you can propose moments. You still cannot
see the picture: say so once.

1. **Transcribe**: `transcribe` (language, pack as above), poll
   `get_transcribe_status`.
2. **Read**: `get_transcript` (default text; page with `offset` until
   there is no `nextOffset`). A 25-minute talk is one or two answers.
3. **Propose 3–5 moments**, each 20–60 s, that stand on their own: a
   complete thought, a story with its ending, a strong claim, a funny
   exchange. Give for each the times as m:ss, a one-line reason, and the
   first words, for example: "12:31–13:08 — why sourdough fails the first
   time ('The mistake everyone makes…')". Start at the start of a sentence
   and end at the end of one: use `find_in_transcript` or
   `get_transcript` with `format: "words"` around the spot for the exact
   times, and leave about 300 ms before the first word and after the last.
4. **Wait for their choice.** They may change times or drop some.
   `suggest_shorts` does the splitting into complete passages for you
   (see "From a long video to a publishing kit").
5. **For each approved moment**: `make_short` with `from_ms`, `to_ms` and a
   `name` (`caption_style: "short"` and a `title` make it look like a short). It copies the project, keeps only that part (titles, captions
   and linked voices follow), makes it upright 9:16 at 1080p and adds
   captions from the speech. The long project is not changed.
6. Upright framing: `set_clip_properties` `"all"` on the short with
   `fit: "blur"`, or `"fill"` + `position_x` after asking where the person
   is in the picture.
7. **Export** each short (`export_video`, `quality: "small"` suits phones),
   one at a time: start, poll `get_export_status` to the end, then the
   next. Tell them where the files are and ask them to watch each once.

No transcript possible (no speech program)? Then work from their times
("12:30–13:10") or from the long pauses `remove_silences` `dry_run: true`
lists, and say that you are choosing blind.

## From a long video to a publishing kit

The whole road from a finished edit to "here is the folder, paste this".
You do the picking where you can; the owner decides at every step marked
ASK. ClipForge never uploads: the owner posts the files themselves.

1. **Clean-up** first (the recipes above: silences, fillers, sound), on
   the long video. Shorts are cut from the cleaned video.
2. **Text**: `transcribe` (or, when the project already has captions,
   skip it: `get_captions` reads them and `suggest_shorts` works from
   them).
3. **Chapters**: `make_chapters` with `dry_run: true`. Rewrite the names
   from what each part is about (keep the times), show the list, then
   save it with `set_publishing_notes` `chapters`.
4. **Publishing notes**. Read the talk, then ASK: offer THREE titles and
   let the owner pick. Write a description of two or three plain
   sentences and 5-10 hashtags, show them, and save with
   `set_publishing_notes`. A `short_title` only if the shorts need
   another one.
5. **Candidates**: `suggest_shorts` (page through it). If the owner
   marked moments in the app, `list_markers` first: those are theirs.
6. **Pick 3-5** by these rules, and say why in one line each:
   - a HOOK in the first 3 seconds: a question, a surprising claim, a
     number, the middle of a story - not "so, as I was saying";
   - ONE idea, not three;
   - a COMPLETE thought: it starts at the start of a sentence and ends
     at the end of one (`suggest_shorts` only offers such passages);
   - it ENDS ON A POINT: a punchline, an answer, a conclusion - not on a
     trailing "and then…";
   - NO inside references: nothing that needs what was said earlier
     ("as I said", "that thing", a name never introduced);
   - 20-45 seconds is the sweet spot; never over 60 for all three sites.
   The signals are hints, not a score: "starts with a question" is a hook
   only if the question is interesting. You cannot see the picture: say
   so once.
7. ASK: "Which of these should I make?" Wait. They may change or drop
   some.
8. **Make them**: for each chosen one `make_short` with `from_ms`,
   `to_ms`, `caption_style: "short"`, `snap_to_speech: true`, a `name`,
   and a `title` of a few words (the hook). Read the warnings in the
   answer (too long, mid-sentence) and fix before going on. Upright
   framing: `set_clip_properties` `"all"` with `fit: "blur"`, or `"fill"`
   + `position_x` after asking where the person is.
9. **Export the kit**: one `export_kit` with the long video
   (`destinations`: `["youtube", "podcast"]`, plus `"whatsapp"` if they
   send it to family) and `projects`: each short with `["shorts"]`.
   Poll `get_export_status` to the end.
10. **Hand over**: the folder, the files by name, and what to paste
    where: "<Project> - text.txt has the title, description, hashtags
    and chapters; the YouTube file goes to YouTube Studio > Create >
    Upload; each Short goes to YouTube Shorts / Instagram Reels / TikTok
    with its own title". Pass on any warning with "check the site". Ask
    them to watch each file once before posting.

## Finished look

- `set_project_settings` `fade_in_ms: 1000`, `fade_out_ms: 1500`.
- One mood for all clips: `set_clip_properties` `"all"` with a `filter`
  ("warm", "cool", "black_and_white", "vintage", "vivid") — dry run first,
  and ask them to look at it; you cannot judge colours.
- Match the clips to one they like: `copy_clip_settings` from that clip to
  `["all"]`.
- Start one clip from black: `fade_in_ms` on that clip
  (`set_clip_properties`).
